    <h2>44 result(s)</h2>
    <div id="decisia-results">
<div class="documentList">
    <ul class="collectionItemList list-expanded">
    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">1.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/coa/coa/en/item/1778/index.do?q=%22Negligence+Act%22+%22jointly+and+severally%22">Sullivan (Estate of) v. Bond</a></span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2001-08-02</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Decisions of the Court of Appeal</span>
                    - <span>Civil</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Thus, in subsection (a)(i) of s. 267.7(1), the use of the words "having regard to s. 267.5" is a clear indication that s. 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b> is not to be applied in determining the amount for which protected and unprotected defendants are to be held <b>jointly and severally</b> liable to plaintiffs. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp; section 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b>. If section 267.5 did not exist, protected defendants would be liable for the plaintiff's entire losses, and the <b>Negligence Act</b> would govern the <b>joint and several</b> liability of all defendants and the liability of the defendants to make contribution and indemnification as amongst themselves. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp; otherwise be liable under s. 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b>. Accordingly, I do not share Dyson J.'s view that the extent of an unprotected defendant's liability is governed, without exception, by s. 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b>, such that unprotected defendants remain liable on a <b>joint and several</b> basis "to pay any shortfall 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
        <div class="documents">
                <a target="_blank" href="/coa/coa/en/1778/1/document.do" title="Download the PDF version" class="matomo_download"><span class="decisia-icon icon-decisia-file-pdf"></span></a>
                <a href='javascript:void(0)' class='expand-result' title='Collapse result'></a>
        </div> 
    </div> 
    </div> 
</li>
    <li class="even list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">2.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/coa/coa/en/item/23334/index.do?q=%22Negligence+Act%22+%22jointly+and+severally%22">Cadieux v. Cadieux</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2025 ONCA 405</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2025-06-04</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Decisions of the Court of Appeal</span>
                    - <span>Civil</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    [25] The appellants rely on Sharpe J.A.’s comment at para. 52 that “[t]he situation as among wrongdoers who are all sued and all found to be <b>jointly and severally</b> liable ... may be different”. This, however, does not apply here as the settling defendant in this case, Ottawa, will not be <b>jointly and severally</b> liable with the&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[26] Acknowledging that Ontario’s <b>Negligence Act</b> does not explicitly provide for a proportionate reduction, the appellants argue that it is available as a form of restitution rooted in equity. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[3] Saskatchewan’s Contributory <b>Negligence Act</b>, RSS 1978, c C-31, as an example, expressly provides for the kind of proportionate reduction that the appellants seek here: see s. 3.1(2). 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
