    <span class="digits">8.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/coa/coa/en/item/7386/index.do?q=%22Negligence+Act%22+%22jointly+and+severally%22">Misko v. John Doe</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2007 ONCA 660</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2007-09-25</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Decisions of the Court of Appeal</span>
                    - <span>Civil</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    [10] The position of the appellant, Liberty Mutual, is based primarily on the <b>Negligence Act</b>, R.S.O. 1990, c. N.1. Section 1 of that Act provides that where damages &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[18] Section 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b> changed the common law and allows for contribution and indemnity among tortfeasors. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Therefore, when applying the section to any specific action, it is understood that <b>joint and several</b> liability to the plaintiff can and will attach only to a party defendant, although others who may also have been at fault could potentially have been found <b>jointly and severally</b> liable had they been sued by the plaintiff. 
                </p>
