<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">9.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/coa/coa/en/item/3802/index.do?q=%22Negligence+Act%22+%22jointly+and+severally%22">Renaissance Leisure Group Inc. v. Frazer</a></span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2004-08-27</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Decisions of the Court of Appeal</span>
                    - <span>Civil</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    The <b>joint and several</b> liability of Renaissance and Frank Chapple amounted to $521,043.25 (65 per cent of the damages). &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[47] Second, and importantly, the comments in Martin that triggered the trial judge’s reconsideration of his initial apportionment ruling were made in the context of an action commenced under s. 1 of the <b>Negligence Act</b>, which is concerned with <b>joint and several</b> liability among party defendants for damages sustained by a &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;As Frazer is not <b>jointly and severally</b> liable to Mellanby, the reasoning in Martin has no application. 
                </p>
